\" stroke=\"var(--color-fg-primary)\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" class=\"icon-noB79L\"><g><path d=\"M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882\"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex=\"0\" type=\"button\" class=\"pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image buttonBase-GK1x3M\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2 icon-noB79L\"><polyline points=\"15 3 21 3 21 9\"></polyline><polyline points=\"9 21 3 21 3 15\"></polyline><line x1=\"21\" x2=\"14\" y1=\"3\" y2=\"10\"></line><line x1=\"3\" x2=\"10\" y1=\"21\" y2=\"14\"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p></p><p>A week without the JDK in the lead role (JDK 27 ships on September 15 and gets its own edition, relax). Instead, three pieces from the last few weeks that share one question: how much of what runs on your JVM did you actually write. The answer, as usual, is \"less than you think\", but this time I have three very concrete examples, one from each layer.</p><div class=\"subscription-widget-wrap-editor\" data-attrs=\"{&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://www.jvm-weekly.com/subscribe?&quot;,&quot;text&quot;:&quot;Subscribe&quot;,&quot;language&quot;:&quot;en&quot;}\" data-component-name=\"SubscribeWidgetToDOM\"><div class=\"subscription-widget show-subscribe\"><div class=\"preamble\"><p class=\"cta-caption\">Thanks for reading JVM Weekly! Subscribe for free to receive new posts and support my work.</p></div><form class=\"subscription-widget-subscribe\"><input type=\"email\" class=\"email-input\" name=\"email\" placeholder=\"Type your email…\" tabindex=\"-1\"><input type=\"submit\" class=\"button primary\" value=\"Subscribe\"><div class=\"fake-input-wrapper\"><div class=\"fake-input\"></div><div class=\"fake-button\"></div></div></form></div></div><h2>1. Why Arrays.fill is 265 times slower on G1</h2><p>Let me start with <strong>Krzysztof Ślusarski</strong>, who some of you know from his JVM profiling talks and from <a href=\"https://krzysztofslusarski.github.io/\">his tooling around async-profiler</a>, published <a href=\"https://krzysztofslusarski.github.io/2026/08/19/g1barrier.html\">Why is Arrays.fill 265 times slower on G1GC?</a> on August 19. The benchmark in it is only the pretext.</p><p>Ślusarski starts with two arrays of a million references, <code>Arrays.fill</code> on both, once with ParallelGC, once with G1. There is no allocation in the method, so there are no GC cycles during the measurement and \"G1 collects garbage slower\" is out from the start. What comes out is 139 milliseconds against half a millisecond. One caveat the author makes himself: the measurement is from an Apple M4 Max under macOS on JDK 25.0.3, and the price of a memory fence depends heavily on the microarchitecture, so the number on server x86 will be different. The mechanism is the same.</p><div class=\"captioned-image-container\"><figure><div class=\"image-link image2\" data-component-name=\"Image2ToDOM\"><div class=\"image2-inset\"><picture><source type=\"image/webp\" srcset=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!F_T2!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F459730a5-5cf4-4fd9-9f21-e0ebfcaf7a58_1664x184.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!F_T2!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F459730a5-5cf4-4fd9-9f21-e0ebfcaf7a58_1664x184.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!F_T2!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F459730a5-5cf4-4fd9-9f21-e0ebfcaf7a58_1664x184.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!F_T2!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F459730a5-5cf4-4fd9-9f21-e0ebfcaf7a58_1664x184.png 1456w\" sizes=\"100vw\"><img src=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!F_T2!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F459730a5-5cf4-4fd9-9f21-e0ebfcaf7a58_1664x184.png\" width=\"1664\" height=\"184\" data-attrs=\"{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/459730a5-5cf4-4fd9-9f21-e0ebfcaf7a58_1664x184.png&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:false,&quot;imageSize&quot;:&quot;normal&quot;,&quot;height&quot;:184,&quot;width&quot;:1664,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:1664,&quot;bytes&quot;:null,&quot;alt&quot;:&quot;The JMH run: same code, same JDK, same machine.&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;image/png&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:false,&quot;topImage&quot;:false,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:null,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}\" class=\"sizing-normal\" alt=\"The JMH run: same code, same JDK, same machine.\" title=\"The JMH run: same code, same JDK, same machine.\" srcset=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!F_T2!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F459730a5-5cf4-4fd9-9f21-e0ebfcaf7a58_1664x184.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!F_T2!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F459730a5-5cf4-4fd9-9f21-e0ebfcaf7a58_1664x184.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!F_T2!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F459730a5-5cf4-4fd9-9f21-e0ebfcaf7a58_1664x184.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!F_T2!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F459730a5-5cf4-4fd9-9f21-e0ebfcaf7a58_1664x184.png 1456w\" sizes=\"100vw\"></picture><div></div></div></div><figcaption class=\"image-caption\">The JMH run: same code, same JDK, same machine.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The difference comes from the write barrier. A generational GC collecting only the young generation has to know which old objects point into the young one, and scanning the whole old generation is out of the question. Instead the heap is cut into 512-byte cards, the JVM keeps one byte per card, and on every reference store the JIT glues on a couple of instructions that mark the card dirty. That is code you did not write, cannot see in your sources, and which runs on every reference assignment in your application. With ParallelGC it is two instructions:</p><div class=\"captioned-image-container\"><figure><div class=\"image-link image2\" data-component-name=\"Image2ToDOM\"><div class=\"image2-inset\"><picture><source type=\"image/webp\" srcset=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!efjE!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F476f7940-cfaa-4aca-ba1b-32c0f90f2e9b_1664x184.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!efjE!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F476f7940-cfaa-4aca-ba1b-32c0f90f2e9b_1664x184.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!efjE!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F476f7940-cfaa-4aca-ba1b-32c0f90f2e9b_1664x184.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!efjE!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F476f7940-cfaa-4aca-ba1b-32c0f90f2e9b_1664x184.png 1456w\" sizes=\"100vw\"><img src=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!efjE!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F476f7940-cfaa-4aca-ba1b-32c0f90f2e9b_1664x184.png\" width=\"1664\" height=\"184\" data-attrs=\"{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/476f7940-cfaa-4aca-ba1b-32c0f90f2e9b_1664x184.png&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:false,&quot;imageSize&quot;:&quot;normal&quot;,&quot;height&quot;:184,&quot;width&quot;:1664,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:1664,&quot;bytes&quot;:null,&quot;alt&quot;:&quot;The ParallelGC barrier: shift, then store a byte.&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;image/png&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:true,&quot;topImage&quot;:false,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:null,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}\" class=\"sizing-normal\" alt=\"The ParallelGC barrier: shift, then store a byte.\" title=\"The ParallelGC barrier: shift, then store a byte.\" srcset=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!efjE!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F476f7940-cfaa-4aca-ba1b-32c0f90f2e9b_1664x184.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!efjE!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F476f7940-cfaa-4aca-ba1b-32c0f90f2e9b_1664x184.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!efjE!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F476f7940-cfaa-4aca-ba1b-32c0f90f2e9b_1664x184.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!efjE!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F476f7940-cfaa-4aca-ba1b-32c0f90f2e9b_1664x184.png 1456w\" sizes=\"100vw\" loading=\"lazy\"></picture><div></div></div></div><figcaption class=\"image-caption\">The ParallelGC barrier: shift, then store a byte.</figcaption></figure></div><p>With G1 it is twenty, four conditional branches and, on the slow path, a full memory fence (<code>dmb ish</code> on ARM64, <code>lock addl</code> on x86):</p><div class=\"captioned-image-container\"><figure><a class=\"image-link image2 is-viewable-img\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!cyF5!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe6e58705-944b-4dcf-b7c6-6218f210269f_1664x516.png\" data-component-name=\"Image2ToDOM\"><div class=\"image2-inset\"><picture><source type=\"image/webp\" srcset=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!cyF5!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe6e58705-944b-4dcf-b7c6-6218f210269f_1664x516.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!cyF5!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe6e58705-944b-4dcf-b7c6-6218f210269f_1664x516.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!cyF5!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe6e58705-944b-4dcf-b7c6-6218f210269f_1664x516.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!cyF5!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.co